1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,508
On the twenty-second of March 2023, a retired admiral carried a bell onto a warship.

2
00:00:06,508 --> 00:00:08,289
The bell was made in 1961.

3
00:00:08,289 --> 00:00:13,831
It had hung on a ship that was broken up for scrap in 1997 — the first INS Vikrant,

4
00:00:13,831 --> 00:00:18,881
which India bought from Britain, and which served for thirty-six years.

5
00:00:29,433 --> 00:00:34,063
For twenty-six years after that ship died, its bell hung outside a house in Delhi.

6
00:00:34,063 --> 00:00:38,971
Vice Admiral S N Ghormade brought it back and gave it to a new ship with the same name.

7
00:00:38,971 --> 00:00:41,481
Here is what makes that more than sentiment.

8
00:00:41,481 --> 00:00:43,154
The first Vikrant was bought.

9
00:00:43,154 --> 00:00:44,381
This one India built.

10
00:00:44,381 --> 00:00:48,585
And the hardest part of building it was not the part anyone expected.

11
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:53,794
An aircraft carrier is three problems wearing one name.

12
00:00:53,794 --> 00:00:54,730
One: the hull.

13
00:00:54,730 --> 00:01:01,344
A moving steel island, forty-three thousand tonnes of it, that has to survive the sea and its own weight.

14
00:01:01,344 --> 00:01:02,280
Two: the deck.

15
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:03,965
Not the metal — the skill.

16
00:01:03,965 --> 00:01:09,830
Landing a jet on a moving ship is the most demanding routine thing done in military aviation,

17
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:13,749
and a navy that cannot do it owns a very expensive ferry.

18
00:01:17,533 --> 00:01:18,506
Three: the wing.

19
00:01:18,506 --> 00:01:22,625
The aircraft themselves, which is what the entire ship exists to carry.

20
00:01:22,625 --> 00:01:26,115
Hold those three, because India's score is two out of three,

21
00:01:26,115 --> 00:01:30,935
and the one it is missing will sound familiar if you watched the last episode.

22
00:01:33,033 --> 00:01:35,202
The day after Vikrant was commissioned,

23
00:01:35,202 --> 00:01:39,107
the journalist Shekhar Gupta wrote a column in ThePrint that gave three

24
00:01:39,107 --> 00:01:43,011
cheers for the ship and then put three questions to India's leadership.

25
00:01:43,011 --> 00:01:45,288
Does India need aircraft carriers at all?

26
00:01:45,288 --> 00:01:47,295
If it does, what kind, and how many?

27
00:01:47,295 --> 00:01:51,036
And what firepower will fly off them, and where will that come from?

28
00:01:51,036 --> 00:01:56,242
They are the right three questions, so this episode will try to answer them — after one detour,

29
00:01:56,242 --> 00:02:00,012
into why making the steel became a national problem of its own.

30
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:06,084
Start with what it is.

31
00:02:06,084 --> 00:02:10,273
INS Vikrant, commissioned at Kochi on the second of September 2022.

32
00:02:10,273 --> 00:02:17,172
Two hundred and sixty-two metres long, sixty-two metres across, about forty-three thousand tonnes fully loaded,

33
00:02:17,172 --> 00:02:22,400
twenty-eight knots flat out, seven and a half thousand nautical miles of range.

34
00:02:23,533 --> 00:02:28,756
Inside, around two thousand two hundred compartments and a crew of about sixteen hundred,

35
00:02:28,756 --> 00:02:36,531
with a hospital that has operating theatres, an intensive care unit and a CT scanner, because at sea there is nowhere to send anyone.

36
00:02:36,531 --> 00:02:38,804
It carries up to thirty aircraft.

37
00:02:39,933 --> 00:02:47,260
It was designed by the Navy's own Warship Design Bureau and built by Cochin Shipyard, with about seventy-six per cent indigenous content,

38
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:50,657
more than a hundred small and medium firms in the supply chain,

39
00:02:50,657 --> 00:02:54,586
and roughly thirteen thousand people working on it in ancillary industry.

40
00:02:54,586 --> 00:02:57,381
It is the largest warship ever built in India.

41
00:03:00,733 --> 00:03:04,312
Now the part that almost no one outside the industry talks about.

42
00:03:04,312 --> 00:03:07,186
You cannot build a warship from ordinary ship steel.

43
00:03:07,186 --> 00:03:12,337
The hull has to take shock — the whip of a near-miss carried through water — without cracking,

44
00:03:12,337 --> 00:03:16,486
and still be weldable in enormous quantities by ordinary yard workers.

45
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:22,955
That is a specific grade, and in the 2000s India did not make it.

46
00:03:22,955 --> 00:03:26,824
It was imported, and the supply was not something India controlled.

47
00:03:26,824 --> 00:03:31,376
So DRDO, the Steel Authority of India and the Navy developed the grade at home.

48
00:03:31,376 --> 00:03:39,244
The Press Information Bureau's word for the result is worth quoting exactly: it made India "self-sufficient" in warship-grade steel.

49
00:03:41,667 --> 00:03:42,782
Notice the sequence.

50
00:03:42,782 --> 00:03:48,199
Before India could build the ship, it had to build the ability to make the metal the ship is made of.

51
00:03:48,199 --> 00:03:54,448
That is why these programmes take as long as they do, and why they are worth more than the one ship at the end.

52
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:58,107
The build, then.

53
00:03:58,107 --> 00:04:00,948
Ceremonial steel cutting in April 2005.

54
00:04:00,948 --> 00:04:02,937
Keel laid in February 2009.

55
00:04:02,937 --> 00:04:12,170
Launched in August 2013 — and a launch is not a finished ship; it is a hull in the water with years of fitting out still to come.

56
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:16,929
Basin trials of the propulsion and power systems in November 2020.

57
00:04:16,929 --> 00:04:19,289
Sea trials from August 2021.

58
00:04:22,267 --> 00:04:27,061
Delivered on the twenty-eighth of July 2022, commissioned on the second of September.

59
00:04:27,061 --> 00:04:29,570
Seventeen years from the first cut of steel.

60
00:04:29,570 --> 00:04:32,023
That is long, and some of it was avoidable.

61
00:04:32,023 --> 00:04:36,873
It is also roughly what carriers take in countries that have built them for a century,

62
00:04:36,873 --> 00:04:42,414
and India was building the yard capability and the design skill at the same time as the ship.

63
00:04:45,833 --> 00:04:50,516
Look at the front of the ship and the deck curves upwards, like a skateboard ramp.

64
00:04:50,516 --> 00:04:54,408
That is a ski-jump, and it is there because Vikrant has no catapult.

65
00:04:54,408 --> 00:04:59,260
A catapult — steam or electromagnetic — grabs an aircraft and throws it off the deck,

66
00:04:59,260 --> 00:05:03,340
which lets it leave heavy, with full fuel and a full weapons load.

67
00:05:08,867 --> 00:05:14,153
India does not have that technology, and the countries that do have been slow to share it.

68
00:05:14,153 --> 00:05:17,755
So Vikrant uses STOBAR: Short Take-Off But Arrested Recovery.

69
00:05:17,755 --> 00:05:24,853
The jet runs down the deck on its own engine power, the ramp flings it upwards, and it climbs while it builds speed.

70
00:05:27,367 --> 00:05:30,661
Coming back, it catches one of three arrester wires.

71
00:05:30,661 --> 00:05:31,283
It works.

72
00:05:31,283 --> 00:05:38,307
The honest cost is that an aircraft leaving a ski-jump cannot be as heavy as one leaving a catapult — less fuel,

73
00:05:38,307 --> 00:05:39,985
or fewer weapons, or both.

74
00:05:39,985 --> 00:05:44,139
Every carrier is a set of compromises, and that is Vikrant's.

75
00:05:47,433 --> 00:05:49,731
So, the second problem: the deck.

76
00:05:49,731 --> 00:05:55,610
On the sixth of February 2023, a MiG-29K made the first fixed-wing landing on Vikrant,

77
00:05:55,610 --> 00:06:00,813
and the indigenous Light Combat Aircraft, Navy version, landed the same day.

78
00:06:00,813 --> 00:06:05,408
On the twenty-fourth of May 2023, a MiG-29K landed on her at night.

79
00:06:05,408 --> 00:06:11,316
The Navy called it a historic milestone, and that is not a press-release flourish.

80
00:06:12,433 --> 00:06:15,918
A night landing on a carrier means flying onto a dark, moving,

81
00:06:15,918 --> 00:06:21,505
pitching rectangle with almost no visual cues, at an approach speed where there is no time to think.

82
00:06:21,505 --> 00:06:23,330
Navies measure themselves on it.

83
00:06:23,330 --> 00:06:30,190
Within three months of the first daylight landing, Indian pilots were doing it in the dark, on a ship India had just built.

84
00:06:30,190 --> 00:06:32,094
That is problem two, solved.

85
00:06:36,633 --> 00:06:38,175
Which leaves the third.

86
00:06:38,175 --> 00:06:39,074
The aircraft.

87
00:06:39,074 --> 00:06:42,414
Vikrant's fighters are MiG-29Ks, which are Russian.

88
00:06:42,414 --> 00:06:44,726
On the twenty-eighth of April 2025,

89
00:06:44,726 --> 00:06:50,378
India and France signed an inter-governmental agreement for twenty-six Rafale-Marine ai

90
00:06:50,378 --> 00:06:56,329
rcraft — twenty-two single-seat and four twin-seat — with deliveries completed by 2030.

91
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:03,166
The package includes training, simulators, weapons, a Rafale fuselage production facility in India,

92
00:07:03,166 --> 00:07:07,490
maintenance and overhaul facilities for engines, sensors and weapons here,

93
00:07:07,490 --> 00:07:11,768
and technology transfer to integrate Indian weapons on the aircraft.

94
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:21,021
That is a serious industrial deal, and it is still a purchase.

95
00:07:21,021 --> 00:07:27,803
India's own answer is the Twin Engine Deck Based Fighter, being developed for exactly this job, and it is years away.

96
00:07:27,803 --> 00:07:31,826
And it is not only the aircraft: as Gupta pointed out in that column,

97
00:07:31,826 --> 00:07:36,206
the gas turbines that drive the ship are imported American GE engines.

98
00:07:37,333 --> 00:07:42,644
So: India built the ship, India can fly from the ship, and the thing that flies is bought.

99
00:07:42,644 --> 00:07:47,021
If that sounds like the Tejas story one deck higher, it is the same story.

100
00:07:47,021 --> 00:07:49,472
The hull is the easy part to be proud of.

101
00:07:49,472 --> 00:07:52,865
The things that go on it are where dependence lives.

102
00:07:57,300 --> 00:08:01,354
So, why spend forty-three thousand tonnes and two decades on this at all?

103
00:08:01,354 --> 00:08:06,559
Because an air force flying from land can only reach so far, and the runway stays where it is.

104
00:08:06,559 --> 00:08:13,817
A carrier is an airfield that moves — one that can be somewhere the day after a decision, rather than the year after a treaty.

105
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:20,509
It also does the quiet work: disaster relief, evacuations, showing up.

106
00:08:20,509 --> 00:08:24,386
But reach has a radius, and that is where the argument gets sharp.

107
00:08:24,386 --> 00:08:32,893
In Gupta's column the analyst Ashley Tellis puts it bluntly: within about a thousand kilometres of the peninsula you do not need a carrier at all,

108
00:08:32,893 --> 00:08:37,476
because shore-based aircraft with air-to-air refuelling already cover it.

109
00:08:41,433 --> 00:08:46,082
Carriers earn their keep further out — off East Africa, or in Southeast Asia.

110
00:08:46,082 --> 00:08:49,181
So the question is not whether carriers are useful.

111
00:08:49,181 --> 00:08:52,161
It is how far from home India intends to operate.

112
00:08:52,161 --> 00:08:55,500
And that is a political decision, not a naval one.

113
00:08:59,967 --> 00:09:01,693
So, Gupta's first question.

114
00:09:01,693 --> 00:09:03,728
Does India need carriers at all?

115
00:09:03,728 --> 00:09:11,311
He calls this the most enduring argument in naval warfare, and he gives it its proper name: sea control versus sea denial.

116
00:09:11,311 --> 00:09:17,160
Sea control is a carrier — you hold an area of ocean and everything in it answers to you.

117
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:29,036
Sea denial is a submarine — you cannot hold the sea, but you can make it unusable for someone else.

118
00:09:29,036 --> 00:09:36,219
In the Cold War the West bought carriers; the Soviet Union, which could not afford that race, bought submarines instead.

119
00:09:36,219 --> 00:09:37,528
Submarines hide.

120
00:09:38,667 --> 00:09:45,784
Carriers are seen, and being seen is precisely their purpose — which is their strength and their exposure in the same sentence.

121
00:09:45,784 --> 00:09:49,921
And the choice is not abstract, because the money is the same money.

122
00:09:52,400 --> 00:10:00,296
Lieutenant General Prakash Menon, writing in ThePrint the same week, argued that India's submarine arm has fallen badly behind:

123
00:10:00,296 --> 00:10:06,280
a 1999 plan for twenty-four conventional submarines, Project 75 running nearly two decades late,

124
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:10,844
and a force of sixteen conventional boats whose lives are being extended.

125
00:10:10,844 --> 00:10:14,290
Every rupee of carrier is a rupee not spent there.

126
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:19,411
Which brings us to Gupta's second question: what kind, and how many.

127
00:10:19,411 --> 00:10:24,585
India operates two carriers — Vikramaditya, bought from Russia and rebuilt, and Vikrant.

128
00:10:24,585 --> 00:10:29,410
The Navy has long said it wants three, so two are available while one is in refit,

129
00:10:29,410 --> 00:10:33,663
because a navy with two carriers does not always have two carriers.

130
00:10:43,367 --> 00:10:44,810
That is not theoretical.

131
00:10:44,810 --> 00:10:51,508
In 1965 a Pakistani force shelled Dwarka and no Indian carrier answered, because the first Vikrant was in dry dock.

132
00:10:51,508 --> 00:10:56,936
Menon makes that case for a third ship and adds another: stop building carriers and the yard,

133
00:10:56,936 --> 00:11:01,683
the designers and the skills disappear, so the next one starts from nothing.

134
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:12,807
A second indigenous carrier, IAC-2, was reported in November 2023 to have in-principle clearance from the Defence Procurement Board — around forty-five thousand tonnes,

135
00:11:12,807 --> 00:11:19,679
about forty thousand crore rupees, and a repeat order of Vikrant rather than a bigger, catapult-equipped ship.

136
00:11:33,433 --> 00:11:34,955
That choice is contested.

137
00:11:34,955 --> 00:11:41,568
Tellis's view, in Gupta's column, is that India has chosen small carriers, heavy on crew and light on firepower,

138
00:11:41,568 --> 00:11:47,771
and that on American experience a carrier wants to be sixty-five thousand tonnes before the numbers work.

139
00:11:47,771 --> 00:11:52,636
A repeat order is cheaper, faster, and uses a design the yard has built once.

140
00:11:53,767 --> 00:11:59,661
A bigger ship would carry more and launch it heavier — and would take another two decades to learn.

141
00:11:59,661 --> 00:12:04,495
As of the twenty-first of November 2026, no contract has been announced publicly.

142
00:12:04,495 --> 00:12:11,397
The argument inside the system is the one above: how many carriers, how big, and what else the money would buy.

143
00:12:19,300 --> 00:12:22,154
So, as of the twenty-first of November 2026.

144
00:12:22,154 --> 00:12:29,448
The ship: commissioned September 2022, about seventy-six per cent indigenous, the largest warship India has built.

145
00:12:29,448 --> 00:12:37,548
The deck: fixed-wing day and night landings done, the carrier working up through air certification and flight integration.

146
00:12:39,867 --> 00:12:46,834
The wing: MiG-29K in service, twenty-six Rafale-Marine on contract with deliveries completed by 2030,

147
00:12:46,834 --> 00:12:50,249
the indigenous deck-based fighter in development.

148
00:12:50,249 --> 00:12:54,416
The steel: made in India, which it was not twenty years ago.

149
00:12:54,416 --> 00:13:00,453
The third carrier: cleared in principle as reported in 2023, no contract announced.

150
00:13:02,067 --> 00:13:08,444
If you remember one thing, make it the three parts of a carrier, in the order India solved them.

151
00:13:08,444 --> 00:13:08,904
Steel.

152
00:13:08,904 --> 00:13:09,299
Deck.

153
00:13:09,299 --> 00:13:09,693
Wing.

154
00:13:09,693 --> 00:13:11,205
India makes the steel.

155
00:13:11,205 --> 00:13:12,980
India has earned the deck.

156
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:19,592
The wing is still arriving — some of it bought, some of it being designed, none of it finished.

157
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:25,700
Six cards on the wall.

158
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:33,794
Next on India's Defence, Decoded: Pinaka, and how a rocket that goes where it is pointed became a rocket that goes where it is told.

159
00:13:33,794 --> 00:13:37,563
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

